n the case of Cassandra  and the unhatched components you can use  to control how data is ordered on disk  for doing scans for anyone is used  Cassandra  this is again really similar to the  notion of partition keys and clustering  or partitioned columns and clustering  columns the data is organized into  different column families within column  families excuse me we have columns sorry  we call these we refer to the column  names as qualifiers again this is same  as BigTable and where are where a data  model diverges so again this so far this  has been pretty similar it's what you  get in Cassandra our data model differs  somewhat from Cassandra you know we have  built-in support for time series data in  Cassandra if you're storing time series  data you'll probably add one clustering  column somewhere in your definition of  your column family  the timestamp in it whereas in qiji we  provide native support for the notion of  timestamp versions of different columns  and again this is this is taken from  from BigTable finally the data we store  in every column consists of complex data  types we use Avro here for serializing  and deserializing the data that we store  in Cassandra Avro is another one of the  open source projects that I referenced  earlier in the talk so these so rather  than just storing like a single string  or a single integer in a column you can  store these complex types that consist  of I can consist of maps and lists and  nested versions of these and enumerated  types finally we have a notion of  locality groups in key G again these are  taken from BigTable and they allow a  user when he specifies the layout of his  table to group together column families  that are accessed together in that we'd  like to have stored on disk together so  let's say for example we're building an  application and we have some set of  column families that we're only going to  access for some kind of batch jobs and  we also have a set of column families  that will access only for some kind of  real time operation that make be  sensible then so we probably want to  store these separately we might want to  have some different attributes for them  whether they're cached for example  whether they're compressed and to do so  we can specify different locality groups  and then put all of our batch column  families at one all of our real time  ones in another and then specify  attributes like that the batch stuff we  might want to keep compressed and on  disk the real time column families we  probably want to keep cached for example  so these locality groups give us a nice  separation between how we want to  organize our data logically in column  families and columns and how we wish to  organize it physically on disk with  locality groups  finally in qiji we have the notion of a  row as the unit of transactional  consistency so we support atomic reads  and comparison sets across rows so now  I'll talk about how we map this data  model onto Cassandra to begin with we  map locality groups to column families  this makes sense I and those of you you  know using Cassandra know that we given  column family we store contiguously on  disk so made me a call or excuse me a  locality group to a column family makes  sense then the entity ID we map to the  primary key of our column family so the  hash components become the partition  columns and the UH gnash components  become clustering columns likewise the  family the qualifier and the version we  also mapped the clustering columns and  finally the data that we store in  Cassandra we store as blobs because  we're using Avro to serialize all the  data that we're storing to give another  look at how this qiji to cassandra  mapping goes this is my artist  interpretation of a single row with a  couple column families in Cassandra so  here we can see the hashed component of  one of our entity IDs we map to the the  row key for our Cassandra row on this  will determine what replica node it goes  on and so on and all of those clustering  columns we described as you know those  of you worked a lot with cql or familiar  those get mapped to composite column  names in Cassandra so again we'll see  everything ordered by first the  unhatched components of our entity ID  and then the column family the column  name in the time stamp and if we had a  couple of different locality groups in  our key G table then here we'd have a  couple of column families in Cassandra  so a few implementation notes on how we  built the Cassandra version of key G we  use the data stacks Java driver so we  did 100% of the implementation with this  we didn't use the thrift API at all we  built it on top of Cassandra 2.6 like  2.7 just came out so probably upgrade  soon we made pretty extensive use of the  async API in the Java driver which was  pretty awesome and I'll describe why we  need that in a minute we also built a  new MapReduce input format that has a  number of features that weren't present  in the in the stock input format that  comes with Cassandra mainly because we  need to support Hadoop 2 but there are a  couple other things we added as well so  now let's talk about some of the issues  with this mapping and how we've  addressed them the biggest issue is that  qiji allows us to allows operations that  operate at the same time on multiple  locality groups so we can have remember  this locality a locality group and qiji  which can contain multiple tables or  multiple column families maps to a  single column family in Cassandra so if  we have a read in key G that goes across  multiple locality groups in the same row  that means we need to have a read in  Cassandra that goes across multiple  column families yes and to implement  this we just have to issue multiple  reads in Cassandra since there's no  mechanism to read from multiple column  families at the same time and this is  one instance of where the async API in  the Java driver really came in handy  because we do a lot of reads real  quickly I get the results back together  this is also one of the reasons that we  had to develop a new Hadoop input format  because we needed to support you know  our MapReduce jobs being able to issue  multiple reads and then combine them in  our record reader another tricky one is  a compare and set that crosses locality  groups so again when you're designing  your qiji table you probably want to put  you're going to put data that's  typically accessed together in a single  locality group so this shouldn't happen  very often but we allow for it in key G  and for this there's really no solution  we can come up with lot since we didn't  want to use zookeeper or something like  that to provide locks on every single  row in our key G table so in the key G  implementation of Cassandra we don't  allow these compare and sets the cross  locality groups and finally in general  we've sacrificed some of this trent some  of this transactional consistency in a  single key G row we still have it for a  single locality group but if you if you  do a read to a row in key G in the  meantime someone could issue a write to  one of those locality groups or someone  could issue a right to multiple locality  groups and your results could be you  could have the old data for one locality  group in the new data for another on so  this is something that we've just chosen  to sacrifice in the notion of better  performance another issue is filters so  HBase provides a very rich set of  user-defined server-side filters the  kind of equivalent in cql is to use  where clauses in your cql queries and  these allow for fairly rich filtering  but it's not quite as powerful as what  we get in HBase so if a user in a qiji  data request chooses the filter based on  some timestamp range or some column  qualifier range those we can translate  to where clauses pretty easily  but if a user has a request in which he  specifies a regular expression on a  column qualifier for example in that  case we just have to do the the  filtering on the client side so you  start looking at some ways to do more of  this filtering on the server in  Cassandra if anyone here has any any  suggestions I'd love to talk to you  after the talk but for now we just issue  some of this filtering on the clients  and really just have to educate our  users about what kind of filters are  very efficient and what art finally  there's an interesting difference in how  we translate these entity IDs with  hashed and unhatched components in the  HBase version of qiji versus the  cassandra version of kiichi so let's say  that we've defined in any excuse me an  entity ID for our application with the  state in the city and the name of our  users maybe we choose to hash the city  and state so we want to distribute our  data among all of our replica nodes  based on the the city and state a users  in and then we leave the username  unhatched so we want to be able to  perform scans over users who are in the  same city in the HBase implementation of  qiji we map this entire entity ID to the  HBase row key we in HBase the row keys  are just byte arrays so we can hash some  components of the any ID leave some of  them on hashed in HBase will order all  this data in lexographic order of the  entity ID in Cassandra on the other hand  we get very wide rows remember we're  mapping these unhatched components of  the entity ID to the clustering columns  in Cassandra so we end up with rows that  are so the rows are dictated by the hash  components which map to our partitioning  columns and then  the rest of the NTID Maps these  clustering columns and this gives us the  functionality that we want because we  can now perform a scan through all of  the different users who live in the same  city and state but it does provide some  limitations that our users need to be  oops  need to be aware of namely that we could  run into limits on the size of one of  these rot wide rows by which I believe  contain two billion elements in them so  this again is not not a big issue but  it's just something that users of the  Kassandra version of qiji need to be  aware of yes alright so the status of  the project we have an alpha version of  key G schema that's available now you  can this URL is a little long so I'll  send this out to the the list or post it  on the website for the meetup but you  can download this now we have a version  of our key G bento box that supports  Cassandra so you can download a tarball  just unpack it run one command and have  a working cluster on your locally on  your machine with Hadoop zookeeper and  Cassandra we have a little tutorial that  lets you build a phonebook that uses a  key a Cassandra key G table so you can  read data to it sorry write data to it  read it back delete entries and so on  over the next quarter  we'll add support for all of the other  components in the key G ecosystem to run  on top of Cassandra the majority of the  work in modifying key G to work on top  of Cassandra was building this Cassandra  version of key G schema in particular  the next quarter we're going to  concentrate on producing a Cassandra  version of key G Express again this is a  really neat tool that lets you very  easily build complicated chains of  MapReduce jobs finally we will begin to  expose some of the Cassandra specific  features to our key G to key G users for  example to allow them when they're  issuing reads or writes to specify  consistency levels  so it's a recap we talked about three  things you need to do to build a big  data application the first was that have  a way to get data in and out of your  database from your application on to  this end we have key G rests and key G  MapReduce the second was to enable your  data scientists to inspect the data and  key G and then use it to gain some  insights and develop and train some  machine learning models for this we have  key G hive key G MapReduce and key G  Express and then finally we need a way  to apply these models in real time to  manage all the models that our data  scientists are building to be able to  deploy them online manage which ones are  active and which ones are not  and for this we have the model  repository and key G scoring so just to  wrap up I want to say thanks to the  Cassandra community for newbies like me  to Cassandra it was really useful to  have all of this information online  everybody who's on the mailing list for  developers and users are really helpful  especially as we were developing this  new loop input format for Cassandra and  also just the amount of videos and  slides and so on that were online from  events like this where made it really  easy to get get up to speed quickly  finally I if you want to get started and  try it now the first URL you can get  access to fully functional bento box and  lots of tutorials and user guides for  all the components I just described and  in the second URL is for the Cassandra  version of the bento box which has  support for schema and a Cassandra  specific tutorial
 

